  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 341 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAmplified and positively tilted ridge axis currently extends from \nthe Rockies into the Canadian Prairie Provinces with a broad 594dam \nhigh centered over the southwestern CONUS at the 500mb level. There \nis a stronger PV anomaly off the Pacific Northwest that has resulted \ntroughing in the northwest CONUS, and this will eventually work to \nprovide more activity by the the middle of the next work week. Over \nthe next 24 to 36 hours, this pattern will maintain mid and upper-\nlevel northerly flow and dominant dAVA. Strong subsidence in this \nregime will continue to strengthen a broad anti-cyclone across much \nof the Central CONUS. For today and Monday, temperatures will remain \nin the 80s, with fairly low humidity as most of the Gulf moisture \nwill not have a route into the lower Missouri River Valley during \nthis time. Depending on how strong this surface anticyclone becomes, \nwe  may see a slightly stronger pressure gradient develop while the \ncenter of it is over the Central Plains, which could give us wind \ngusts at times around 15 MPH. This currently looks more favored for \nMonday afternoon than it does today. \n\nTemperatures will steadily climb back into the 90s as the week \nprogresses. This will be caused by a series of vort maxima emanating \nfrom the Pacific Northwest ejecting into the northern Plains and \nCanadian Prairie Provinces, that will force the ridge axis further \neast, while also starting to deamplify the pattern for the Central \nCONUS. This will turn flow from 850mb and below to the surface \nsoutherly through the week, introducing a WAA regime. Current \nguidance still keeps the strongest dCVA well north of the area, \nwhich overall will limit forcing across our area. For Tuesday, low \nlevel flow is progged to have dominant southwesterly component to it \nwhich will push temperatures upward. By late Tuesday Night, as the \nridge axis breaks down, low-level winds back to more a southerly \ncomponent which will open up Gulf moisture transport for the lower \nMissouri River Valley. As a result, humidity will begin to increase, \nwhile temperatures still are forecast to remain in the 90s. As the \nPV anomaly over the Pacific Northwest continues to send vort maxima \neastward and a trough attempts to dig into the Rockies, the Central \nPlains and Midwest will start to see an upward trend of dCVA which \nwill attempt to generate a Lee surface cyclone across the area early \nWednesday, eventually pushing eastward into eastern Kansas and \nwestern Missouri. With better lift present and increasing moisture, \nrain showers and thunderstorm probabilities will increase Wednesday \nevening through Thursday morning. Ensemble probabilities are \nfavoring areas north of Hwy. 36 over a 50-60 percent chance of \nexceeding at 0.10 inches of QPF, though mentionable probabilities do \nextend all the way southward past the Interstate 70 corridor. With \nthe deepening surface cyclone, notable CAPE axis does develop across \nthe area, and should at least prime the environment for general \nthunderstorms. What is uncertain at this time, is how strong the \ndCVA will be, what the H5 height gradient and associated jet stream \naction will look like. This has implications for the shear profiles. \nTherefore, uncertain if this will pose any kind of severe potential. \nDeterministic model guidance continues to depict the better mid and \nupper-level kinematic support in the northern CONUS. \n\nFor next weekend, the pattern looks rather unsettled across the area \nwhich could lead to multiple rounds of rain showers. Currently, \nensemble probabilities for any specific time period next weekend are \nfairly low, as solutions amongst individual members vary widely in \nwhen forcing arrives. This is also leading to larger inner-quartile \nspread with respect to high temperatures toward the end of the 7 day \nforecast period.",
